{Towards the close of the Northern Open Championship at Whitby, I asked round among the players which was their most interesting game. Here is one of those which were nominated; I need not say who by.} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. Bxc4 c5 6. O-O a6 7. a4 b6 8. Qe2 Bb7 9. Rd1 Qc7 10. Nc3 Nbd7 11. e4 cxd4 12. Nxd4 Ne5 {It must be extremely rare for Black to castle queen’s side in the Queen’s Gambit Accepted. Here, of course, the move holds a direct threat. Golombek in his report to The Times, ascribed his loss to trying to avoid a draw. Our readers might like to play the sleuth and try to find out which juncture of the game he was referring to.} 13. Bb3 Bc5 14. g3 Neg4 15. h3 O-O-O 16. hxg4 Rxd4 17. Rxd4 Bxd4 18. Be3 Bxc3 19. Rc1 Nxe4 20. Bd4 ({It is suddenly apparent that White has gone astray . . . Black just keeps on capturing; for instance,} 20. f3 {, on which Golombek had probably relied as an answer to this, would let in} Qxg3+ {with far too much play for Black.}) 20... Bxd4 21. Rxc7+ Kxc7 22. Bc2 Nd6 23. Bd3 a5 24. g5 h6 25. g6 fxg6 26. Kh2 Rf8 27. f4 Bd5 28. Bxg6 Nf5 29. Qd3 Bc5 30. Qc3 Rf6 31. Bh5 Kd7 32. Qd3 g6 33. Bd1 Nd6 34. Bg4 Ke7 35. Qa6 Bb7 36. Qa7 Rf8 {Source: Illustrated London News, 31 August 1963} 0-1

